offlinecompiler: stop before the code buffer is replaced.

0016 stopped a cache that spans a code buffer swap from being written and
published. This stops the swap happening during generation at all, so a binary
too large for one buffer gets a partial cache instead of none.

When emission fills the buffer, JIT.cpp calls ClearCodeCache, which installs a
fresh buffer and restarts the offset at zero, while relocations are deliberately
kept across that point -- so everything recorded before the swap names an offset
into a buffer that is gone. Measured on Resident Evil Requiem: ~74 seconds and
~350 MB per launch, discarded on load every time, in every session of the
corpus.

The buffer cannot be made bigger. MAX_CODE_SIZE's own comment says why: "we
don't want to move above 128MB atm because that means we will have to encode
longer jumps". That is the +/-128 MB reach of an AArch64 b/bl. Caching beyond
one buffer needs far-branch veneers at every cross-buffer edge -- JIT work with
a permanent cost on those edges, not a patch.

Two limitations stated rather than discovered later. The blocks that make it in
are the ones compiled first, and BlockList is walked in address order rather
than by how often a block runs, so the cached slice is arbitrary rather than
hot; ordering the codemap by frequency is what would fix that. And the margin is
a bound on one block's translated size, not a measurement -- a block larger than
it still reaches the swap, where 0016's checks catch it.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT SETTLE. A cache is only sound if the guest bytes it was
generated from are the bytes that run. For a title that decrypts its own code
that is not guaranteed, and 0011 recorded it as an open correctness problem. It
is substantially mitigated in practice by FEX's own SMC tracking -- a write to a
code page invalidates the translations for it, cached ones included, which is
the same mechanism observed thrashing 5,600 times on one Requiem page -- but
mitigated is not proven, and a page decrypted before write tracking is armed
would still slip through. Binding a cache to a hash of its source bytes is what
would prove it, and the format has no such field today.

diff --git a/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Core/Context.h b/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Core/Context.h
--- a/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Core/Context.h
+++ b/FEXCore/include/FEXCore/Core/Context.h
@@ -141,6 +141,17 @@
   virtual void FlushAndCloseCodeMap() = 0;
 
   FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY virtual void ClearCodeCache(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread, bool NewCodeBuffer = true) = 0;
+
+  /**
+   * @brief How many bytes remain in the code buffer translated code is being emitted into.
+   *
+   * Vessel: exists so that ahead-of-time generation can stop before the buffer is
+   * replaced. A replacement restarts the buffer offset at zero while relocations
+   * recorded before it keep offsets into the buffer that is now gone, which makes
+   * the whole cache unwritable -- see patches/fex/0016. Nothing at runtime needs
+   * this; the runtime is entitled to swap buffers whenever it likes.
+   */
+  FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY virtual size_t GetCodeBufferFreeBytes() = 0;
   FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY virtual void InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange(uint64_t Start, uint64_t Length) = 0;
   FEX_DEFAULT_VISIBILITY virtual void
   InvalidateThreadCachedCodeRange(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread, uint64_t Start, uint64_t Length) = 0;
diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h
--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h
+++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h
@@ -218,6 +218,14 @@
 
   void OnCodeBufferAllocated(const std::shared_ptr<CPU::CodeBuffer>&) override;
   void ClearCodeCache(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread, bool NewCodeBuffer = true) override;
+
+  // Vessel: see the declaration in the public header. ContextImpl already derives
+  // from SharedCodeBufferManager, so this is the manager's own arithmetic.
+  size_t GetCodeBufferFreeBytes() override {
+    auto Buffer = GetLatest();
+    const size_t Usable = Buffer->UsableSize();
+    return Usable > LatestOffset ? Usable - LatestOffset : 0;
+  }
   void InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange(uint64_t Start, uint64_t Length) override;
   void InvalidateThreadCachedCodeRange(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread, uint64_t Start, uint64_t Length) override;
   FEXCore::Utils::WritePriorityMutex::Mutex& GetCodeInvalidationMutex() override {
diff --git a/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp b/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
--- a/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
+++ b/Source/Tools/FEXOfflineCompiler/Main.cpp
@@ -543,12 +543,54 @@
     fmt::print(stderr, "Compiling code...\n");
 
     FEX_CONFIG_OPT(MaxInst, MAXINST);
+
+    /* Vessel: stop before the code buffer is replaced, not after.
+     *
+     * When emission fills the buffer, JIT.cpp calls ClearCodeCache, which
+     * installs a fresh one and restarts the offset at zero -- while relocations
+     * are deliberately kept across that point. Everything recorded before the
+     * swap then names an offset into a buffer that no longer exists, and the
+     * cache cannot be written at all: 0016 measured this costing ~74 seconds and
+     * ~350 MB per launch on Resident Evil Requiem, every launch, for a file that
+     * was refused on load every time.
+     *
+     * The buffer cannot simply be made bigger. MAX_CODE_SIZE's own comment says
+     * why -- "we don't want to move above 128MB atm because that means we will
+     * have to encode longer jumps" -- which is the +/-128 MB reach of an AArch64
+     * b/bl, not a preference. Caching more than that needs far-branch veneers at
+     * every cross-buffer edge, which is JIT work with a permanent cost on those
+     * edges.
+     *
+     * So a binary whose translated code exceeds one buffer gets a *partial*
+     * cache instead of none. Two honest limitations, both worth knowing before
+     * reading a benchmark: the blocks that make it in are the ones compiled
+     * first, and this walks BlockList in address order rather than by how often
+     * a block runs -- so the slice is arbitrary, not hot. And the margin below is
+     * a bound on one block's translated size, not a measurement; a block larger
+     * than it would still trigger the swap, which 0016's checks then catch.
+     */
+    constexpr size_t BlockSizeMargin = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
+    size_t Compiled = 0, Skipped = 0;
+
     for (auto Addr : BlockList) {
       if (!CTX->CheckIfBlockIsCacheable(*Thread, Addr + SyscallHandler->VAFileStart, MaxInst)) {
         continue;
       }
 
+      if (CTX->GetCodeBufferFreeBytes() < BlockSizeMargin) {
+        ++Skipped;
+        continue;
+      }
+
       CTX->CompileRIP(Thread, Addr + SyscallHandler->VAFileStart);
+      ++Compiled;
+    }
+
+    if (Skipped) {
+      LogMan::Msg::IFmt("Code buffer full after {} blocks; {} left uncached and will be translated at "
+                        "runtime. A single buffer holds 128 MB of translated code, which is the branch "
+                        "range of the instructions that jump between blocks.",
+                        Compiled, Skipped);
     }
 
     auto Filename = fmt::format("{}{}-{:016x}", OutDir, FEXCore::CodeMap::GetBaseFilename(Binary, false), CodeCacheConfigId);
